research/scan/SCAN.md:445:Necessity. Each of the 9 rows is the unique lookup in a 2-column input (necessity_all_rows_used True). On the sequential left fold, a wrong output changes that sum exactly when it leaves the carry class of the true output. G is its own class; K and P both emit carry 0. There are 5 same-class corruptions (KK->P, KP->P, PK->P, PP->K, GK->P) and 13 carry-crossing corruptions (KK->G, KP->G, KG->K, KG->P, PK->G, PP->G, PG->K, PG->P, GK->G, GP->K, GP->P, GG->K, GG->P). necessity_carry_crossing_changes_sum True. necessity_same_carry_class_preserves_sum True. That second flag is the sequential fold only: the running prefix may change between K and P, and both emit carry 0, so the digits do not change. A parallel schedule is a different parenthesization. Once a row is wrong the operation need not be associative, and Hillis–Steele or Blelloch can disagree with the sequential sum and with bigint. The audit-corrections section recounts how many of the 18 single-entry corruptions do that. Light's test is a different predicate: it flags 14 of the 18 single-entry substitutions and misses 4 (KP->G, KG->K, GK->G, GP->K). Of the ones it misses, the carry-crossing ones still change a sequential sum (KP->G, KG->K, GK->G, GP->K). The 14/18 count is only that single-entry list.
